    "case_summary": "A 63-year-old man with a history of hypertension, hyperlipidemia, and multiple strokes presented with vision loss in the right eye. The patient had persistent symptoms for 1.5 years with visual acuity ranging between 20/100 to 20/200.\n\nOphthalmoscopic examination revealed severe macular changes with associated edema.\n\n- Imaging Studies:    1. Spectral-domain optical coherence tomography findings:       - Initial scan showed persistent central macular thickness of 669.8 [79.3] μm over 12 visits       - Sequential scans documented changes in macular edema over the course of treatment           Image Titles and Descriptions:     Figure: 'Reversible Effect on Cystoid Macular Edema (CME) Secondary to Retinal Vein Occlusion After Stopping Niacin Therapy'    - Shows series of OCT scans demonstrating changes in macular edema under different treatment conditions\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Central Retinal Vein Occlusion (CRVO)\nB. Macular retinal vein occlusion with chronic cystoid macular edema\nC. Branch Retinal Vein Occlusion (BRVO)\nD. Retinal vein occlusion\n",
